jtglobals_x86_64 -- the GH-1034 fixture for a memory limit that must cover flow
following (decompiler/crates/kuna-cli/tests/decompile_budget_cli.rs).

What it is
  A 39,472-byte x86-64 ELF built from jtglobals_x86_64.c, which is generated C:
  `big` makes 400 calls, each between a read and a write of one of 400 writable
  globals, and ends in a 16-case switch compiled to a jump table. Recovering the
  table runs the jump-table sub-pipeline over everything before it, which
  guards every global at every call, while `kuna decompile`'s `load addr`
  follows flow and before the decompile drive starts.

Why it is vendored
  The memory the recovery takes depends on the code layout, so the binary is
  checked in rather than built at test time.

Where it came from
  gcc 11.4.0 (Ubuntu 11.4.0-1ubuntu1~22.04.3), GNU ld 2.38, x86-64 Linux:

      gcc -O2 -fno-pie -no-pie -fno-inline -o jtglobals_x86_64 jtglobals_x86_64.c

  sha256 75be111198ac5df026d365d72738af30775b40ee46c1bf1de8fd43fb2c07fc3f.
  Run with no arguments it exits with status 59.
